WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.485
Here's one line of Kotlin.

2
00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:12.932
aisle returns a Flow, a stream of values that arrive one at a time: here, the fruit in one aisle of a grocer's online shop.

3
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.122
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

4
00:00:16.122 --> 00:00:19.477
When this line returns, has the shop sent anything yet?

5
00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:23.564
And if two readers use the same flow, do they share what came back?

6
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.838
Let's step into the kitchen.

7
00:00:28.838 --> 00:00:31.456
A recipe card for lemon cake cooks nothing.

8
00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:35.473
It can sit in a drawer for a year, and the kitchen stays spotless.

9
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:41.247
Only when somebody cooks from it does anything happen, from the top, one step at a time.

10
00:00:41.247 --> 00:00:45.911
And if two people cook from one card, every step happens twice: two cakes.

11
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:46.937
Let's map it.

12
00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:50.276
The card is the flow, and writing it is calling aisle.

13
00:00:50.276 --> 00:00:53.616
Cooking from it is collecting, with collect or toList.

14
00:00:53.616 --> 00:00:57.573
And each person cooking is a collector, with a run of their own.

15
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.667
Now shrink the shop: three pages, two items on each.

16
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:09.144
For every page, the flow prints fetching, gets the page, and calls emit for each item, which hands it to the collector.

17
00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:10.838
First we build the flow.

18
00:01:10.838 --> 00:01:13.841
It prints building, then built, and nothing else.

19
00:01:13.841 --> 00:01:15.680
The shop hasn't heard a thing.

20
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.535
Now we collect it twice.

21
00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:20.329
How many pages does the shop send in all?

22
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:23.599
Six.

23
00:01:23.599 --> 00:01:29.960
The first collect fetches pages 0, 1 and 2, and the second starts again from the top.

24
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.280
Two cooks, one card, two cakes.

25
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:36.949
Now take(3), which collects only the first three items.

26
00:01:36.949 --> 00:01:38.008
How many pages?

27
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:41.288
Two.

28
00:01:41.288 --> 00:01:44.095
The third item, cherries, is on page 1.

29
00:01:44.095 --> 00:01:49.204
Once take has three, it cancels the flow, so page 2 is never asked for.

30
00:01:49.204 --> 00:01:53.379
Nobody wanted the figs and grapes, so nobody fetched them.

31
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.329
A flow like this is called cold.

32
00:01:56.329 --> 00:01:57.993
Building it runs nothing.

33
00:01:57.993 --> 00:02:00.855
Each collector runs it again, from the top.

34
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:02.586
Here the picture breaks.

35
00:02:02.586 --> 00:02:05.055
In a kitchen, a second cake is a treat.

36
00:02:05.055 --> 00:02:10.056
A second run of a flow is just another trip to the shop, and nothing warns you.

37
00:02:10.056 --> 00:02:12.272
Sharing one run is the next lesson.

38
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.505
Flows have more steps, like recipes.

39
00:02:15.505 --> 00:02:21.920
filter and map are intermediate operators: each one returns a new flow at once and runs nothing.

40
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.192
It only writes a line on the card.

41
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.115
Let's run some steps.

42
00:02:26.115 --> 00:02:32.730
The kitchen scale sends 250 grams, minus 40, because nobody zeroed it, and 1,200.

43
00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.201
filter keeps the positive ones, and map turns grams into kilograms.

44
00:02:38.433 --> 00:02:44.435
Which line prints first: emit minus 40, or collect 0.25 kilograms?

45
00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.031
collect 0.25 kilograms.

46
00:02:49.031 --> 00:02:56.118
The first weight goes all the way down before the second is emitted, and minus 40 stops at the filter.

47
00:02:56.118 --> 00:02:59.592
No queues between the steps, and no extra threads.

48
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.815
The steps that cook are terminal operators, such as collect, toList and first.

49
00:03:04.815 --> 00:03:07.579
They start the flow and read it to the end.

50
00:03:07.579 --> 00:03:09.186
Intermediate steps write.

51
00:03:09.186 --> 00:03:10.408
Terminal ones cook.

52
00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:14.387
So far every step ran on the collector's thread.

53
00:03:14.387 --> 00:03:21.285
Now a delivery note arrives, one line per item, and the old reader's readLine blocks, holding its thread.

54
00:03:21.285 --> 00:03:25.227
That belongs on the bench by the front door, Dispatchers.IO.

55
00:03:25.433 --> 00:03:28.719
The obvious fix is withContext around the emit.

56
00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:34.172
Run it, and the flow throws IllegalStateException: Flow invariant is violated.

57
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.255
It has a point.

58
00:03:35.255 --> 00:03:40.213
emit runs the collector's own code, so the collector would be dragged to the bench too.

59
00:03:40.213 --> 00:03:45.855
It's like reading the note on the bench, and making the whole family eat dinner out there with you.

60
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:47.929
The tool is flowOn.

61
00:03:47.929 --> 00:03:56.299
Everything above it, the upstream, runs at the bench in a second coroutine, with a shelf of up to 64 lines between the halves.

62
00:03:56.299 --> 00:03:58.292
Everything below it stays put.

63
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.794
The demo prints each step's thread.

64
00:04:00.794 --> 00:04:04.924
read and parse ran on DefaultDispatcher-worker-1, at the bench.

65
00:04:04.924 --> 00:04:06.956
format and save stayed on main.

66
00:04:06.956 --> 00:04:08.660
The collector never moves.

67
00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:11.221
Last: what if a step fails?

68
00:04:11.221 --> 00:04:16.549
catch is a net under the steps above it: whatever falls from up there lands in it.

69
00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:18.823
onCompletion is the flow's finally.

70
00:04:18.823 --> 00:04:21.163
It runs once, however the flow ends.

71
00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:23.348
Tear the note after two lines.

72
00:04:23.348 --> 00:04:27.509
onCompletion prints the cause, IOException: delivery note torn.

73
00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:32.331
catch emits ABORTED, the collector gets it, and collect returns normally.

74
00:04:32.533 --> 00:04:38.432
Now the note is whole, but saving the eggs fails in the collector: the pantry shelf is full.

75
00:04:38.432 --> 00:04:39.587
Does catch see it?

76
00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:42.757
No.

77
00:04:42.757 --> 00:04:47.890
The failure was below catch, so it went straight to the code that called collect.

78
00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:51.376
onCompletion saw it pass, because it sees every ending.

79
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.730
A net under the stove can't catch a plate dropped in the dining room.

80
00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:02.195
So put work that may fail above catch, and never put a try around emit, which runs the collector's code too.

81
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.873
Here it all is in Kotlin, and in Java with a Reactor Flux, which is cold in the same way.

82
00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:13.992
So, has the shop sent anything when the line returns?

83
00:05:13.992 --> 00:05:18.197
Not until someone collects, and every collector cooks from the top.

84
00:05:18.197 --> 00:05:27.860
Steps write on the card, the terminal one cooks, flowOn moves the steps above it, catch catches what falls from above, and onCompletion sees every ending.

85
00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:28.926
Quick check.

86
00:05:28.926 --> 00:05:33.579
flow A, map B, flowOn IO, filter C, collect D, collected on main.

87
00:05:33.579 --> 00:05:35.297
Where does each one run?

88
00:05:38.267 --> 00:05:40.341
A and B on IO, above flowOn.

89
00:05:40.341 --> 00:05:43.379
C and D stay on main, with the collector.
